.TH SH-FLOW 1
.SH NAME
flow \- shell script interface to flow(2)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load flow

.B id := ${flow new}
.br
.B flow
.I id
.B grid
.I nx ny lx ly viscosity
.RB ( periodic | channel | cavity )
.br
.B flow
.I id
.B init
.I u-expr v-expr
.br
.B flow
.I id
.B lid
.I speed
.br
.B flow
.I id
.B force
.I fx fy
.br
.B flow
.I id
.B step
.I dt
.br
.B flow
.I id
.B run
.I dt nsteps
.br
.B ${flow
.I id
.B at
.I x y
.B }
.br
.B ${flow
.I id
.B profile
.I x n
.B }
.br
.B ${flow
.I id
.B field
.I nx ny
.RI [ scale ]
.B }
.br
.B ${flow
.I id
.BR divergence | energy | maxstep | time | nx | ny
.B }
.br
.B ${flow
.I id
.B courant
.I dt
.B }
.br
.B flow del
.I id...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Flow
makes
.IR flow (2)
usable from
.IR sh (1).
.PP
A flow is a
.BR field ,
not a series, which is the difficulty: there is no curve to hand to a
plot.
Three things come back instead, and between them they are how a flow is
looked at.
.TP
.B Field
a coarse grid of
.I x y dx dy
for a
.B vector
layer \- arrows, which here are right, because a velocity really is an
arrow at a point.
The scale is the caller's, since the right arrow length depends on the
box and on how fast the flow happens to be.
.TP
.B Profile
the velocity across a vertical line, as
.I y u v
for
.I n
points.
That
.I is
a series, and it is what a channel flow is judged by.
.TP
.B At
the velocity at one point, interpolated from the faces around it.
.PP
.B Init
takes two
.IR hoc (2)
expressions in
.I x
and
.IR y ,
one per component, as
.IR sh-optim (1)
takes one: a shell has no functions to give, so the initial field is
compiled from text.
.PP
.B Divergence
is the projection's own residual and belongs at rounding.
.B Maxstep
is the largest stable step and
.B courant
the ratio for a proposed one, which is how to find out before rather
than after.
.SH EXAMPLE
A channel under a steady push settles into a parabola:
.IP
.EX
load std
load flow
f := ${flow new}
flow $f grid 4 16 1.0 1.0 1.0 channel
flow $f init 0 0
flow $f force 2.0 0
flow $f run 0.0009 4500
echo ${flow $f at 0.5 0.5}		# 0.25 0, = gH^2/8nu
echo ${flow $f divergence}		# 0
.EE
.PP
and a Taylor-Green vortex decays at a known rate:
.IP
.EX
g := ${flow new}
flow $g grid 32 32 6.2832 6.2832 0.05 periodic
flow $g init '0-cos(x)*sin(y)' 'sin(x)*cos(y)'
flow $g run 0.002 250
echo ${flow $g energy}	# 0.9051 of its first value, against exp(-0.1)
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/flow.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-plot (1),
.IR sh-optim (1),
.IR flow (2),
.IR flowtest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A grid too small to have an interior, a negative viscosity, a step far
beyond the stable one, a pressure solve that did not converge: each
fails the command with the message
.IR flow (2)
gives.
.SH BUGS
There is no test for this module in
.BR /appl/cmd .
.IR Flow (2)
underneath it is checked by
.IR flowtest (1);
what is added here is the hoc bridge and the three samplers, exercised
by the examples above and by
.BR /lib/demo/cavitylab.sh .
.PP
.B Profile
takes a vertical line only.
A horizontal one, which is what a boundary layer is read along, needs a
Limbo program.
.PP
The pressure field is not reachable from here at all, so the one thing
the staggered grid exists to get right cannot be looked at.
.PP
A run of any size is slow from a shell, because every step is a
conjugate-gradient solve and the shell adds a command dispatch per
.BR run ,
not per step \- so use
.B run
with a large count rather than looping
.B step
in
.IR sh (1).
